Bathypelagic Stepper
Ingredients: DUB + POST-ROCK + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Harpsichord + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The sound was discovered by deep-sea saturation divers repairing transatlantic copper-clad telegraph cables three miles down on the Porcupine Abyssal Plain.

A suffocating, subterranean collision where the skeletal, tape-delayed rhythm foundations of 1970s Kingston dub collide with the glacial, crescendo-heavy architectures of third-wave post-rock. Looming sub-bass frequencies and metallic spring-reverb snare cracks reverberate like hull groans under three hundred atmospheres of hydrostatic pressure, while an antique baroque harpsichord plucks brittle, microtonal counterpoints through oil-filled subsea acoustic hydrophones and decaying magnetic delay reels.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 64–72 BPM; a cavernous, sub-heavy one-drop half-time pulse punctuated by subterranean kick thuds, muted rim clicks, and delayed high-hats that billow like plumes of geothermal silt.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Aeolian modal frameworks anchored in low D minor and C minor, disrupted by eerie French baroque ornaments, Picardy thirds, and dissonant clusters plucked on wire-wound harpsichord strings.
- Production Signatures: Hydro-acoustic convolution reverb captured inside decommissioned decompression chambers; tape flanging modulated by subsea pressure fluctuations; cascading Roland Space Echo trails deliberately fed back through vintage high-voltage passive equalizers.
- Dynamics: Glacial, monolithic patience; tracks open in cavernous near-vacuum with isolated harpsichord arpeggios and dry bass pulses before slowly accreting dense stratas of bowed baritone guitars and analog tape hiss, detonating in towering waves of saturated, feedback-drenched post-rock catharsis.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement coalesced in the winter of 1978 inside the damp harbor quays of Cobh and the ballast holds of commercial cable-tenders anchored off the Celtic shelf. Saturation diver and amateur organist Cormac Callahan, trapped for twenty-eight days inside a pressurized steel habitat alongside Scottish cable splicers, began piping electrical test signals through a salvaged Flemish harpsichord bolted to the bulkhead. When the copper telegraph lines grounded against underwater hydrothermal vents, the divers listened through intercom headphones as transatlantic Morse hum and ocean current noise intermingled with King Tubby dub tapes smuggled aboard by West Indian rigging engineers. Scene disciples wear rubberized oilskin coats with brass umbilical fittings, gathering in abandoned dry-dock pump houses lit by sodium lamps to witness performances where musicians mix live harpsichord and bass behind pressurized glass partitions.

Typical Instruments: Flemish twin-manual harpsichord (fitted with magnetic pickup strips), 1974 Fender Jazz Bass (flatwound strings through an Ampeg SVT), Roland RE-201 Space Echo, Mutron Bi-Phase, bowed Gibson Les Paul, 100-gallon steel oil drum percussion, Mutron III envelope filter, hydrophone arrays.

