Track 1: Barnacles Chew the Copper Strand
- Story & Context: Three hundred fathoms beneath the Porcupine Bight during the gale of November 1977, diver Maeve O'Driscoll knelt in black silt while her pneumatic shears severed a corrupted transatlantic telegraph cable that had begun broadcasting unauthorized phantom harmonics. Back in the dive bell, the umbilical audio feed picked up the erratic percussion of water jets clattering against her bronze breastplate while Gwilym Rhys tapped nervous staccato fugues on the habitat's bolted harpsichord. Over the speaker crackle and the rushing oxygen mix, O'Driscoll murmured into her mask "the ocean floor has teeth of wire, and it won't give back the cable," right before the high-voltage cable snapped and flooded the telemetry tape with three minutes of pure acoustic whiteout. That corrupted reel was smuggled ashore in an airtight grease tin, becoming the defining bootleg blueprint for the entire bathypelagic underground.
