Track 2: Diver Cormac in the Decompression Bell
- Story & Context: Confined to an eight-by-ten steel compression chamber for three weeks following an emergency ascent off the Fastnet Rock, diver Cormac Callahan spent his waking hours running a magnetic contact microphone across the rivets while listening to pirate radio broadcasts from Dublin on a modified shortwave receiver. He confessed into the chamber logbook that the rhythmic hiss of helium-oxygen scrubbers sounded identical to King Tubby's spring reverbs, prompting him to accompany the ventilation cycles using a miniature tabletop harpsichord assembled from salvaged piano wire and tin solder. His tremulous voice, pitched upward by the helium gas, echoed through the intercom with the heartbreaking refrain "sixteen atmospheres between my ribcage and the pier," while outside on the pier crewmen wept into their tea. When the chamber hatch was finally unbolted, Callahan left behind forty cassette spools labeled with the exact pressure readings of every recorded bar.
